  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday Night through next Thursday) \nIssued at 1036 PM CDT Wed Jul 29 2026\n\nI think models have come into much better agreement since last night \nregarding the arrival of showers and storms for the weekend. Heavy \nconsensus that Friday night will bring the return of wet conditions. \nIt looks like it'll be after sunset and maybe closer to midnight, \nbut definitely before sunrise Saturday as a positively-tilted upper\nlevel low digs into the Ohio and TN valleys. I took a look at \nforecast soundings again tonight and they're still unimpressive \nthrough Saturday with the evolution of the approaching front. \nFropa should occur Saturday afternoon and while we'll be unstable,\nlapse rates are poor (on the order of 6.0-6.3 deg/km). That said,\nwhile the severe threat appears to be very low Saturday \nafternoon, QPFs probs have ticked up since last night. The last 4 \nruns of the NBM have incrementally increased the likelihood of \nseeing greater than 1.5 inches to 40-50% with 2+ inches now up to \n20-30%. ECMWF EFI tables from this morning have also started to \nhighlight our eastern half with a weak signal for excessive \nrainfall, as well. Right now, the good news is, this is focused \neast of I-65 where 1- and 3-hr FFG is higher. However, if this \ntrend persists, we may have to start talking about this a little \nlouder. \n\nRain chances look to remain in the forecast through Sunday as the\nupper low associated with this system digs further into the TN \nvalley. Rainfall amounts look manageable Sunday. The good news, \nit should be even cooler with low to mid 80s accompanying the \nrain.\n\nTemperatures rebound as we move into next week (though still at or \njust below normals) and with general troughiness across the \nsoutheast, diurnal showers and storms may very well remain in the \nforecast through this time next week. If your grass is growing out \nof control, I don't see much help with that any time soon.",
